SECTION 2: AI TOOLS, LLMs AND RAG

## 9. How do you use AI tools in development?

→ I use AI tools to understand unfamiliar code, generate initial implementations, create test cases and explore refactoring options.

→ I treat AI output as a suggestion, not as production-ready code.

→ I validate the output against requirements, edge cases, security rules and performance expectations.

→ I run tests and review the code manually before accepting it, and I never share confidential information with unauthorised tools.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 10. What is the difference between AI assistants and AI agents?

→ An AI assistant mainly responds to user prompts with explanations or suggestions.

→ An AI agent can plan multiple steps and use approved tools to complete a task.

→ For example, an assistant may explain a production error, while an agent with authorised access could collect logs, analyse metrics and prepare an incident summary.

→ Agents require strict permissions, audit logs, guardrails and human approval for risky actions.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 11. What is RAG?

→ RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation.

→ It retrieves relevant information from documents or databases and provides that information to an LLM as context.

→ The LLM generates an answer using the retrieved context, which can reduce hallucinations.

→ RAG is useful when answers must be based on internal documents or frequently updated information.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 12. Explain the RAG workflow

→ First, documents are extracted, cleaned and divided into smaller chunks.

→ An embedding model converts chunks into vectors, which are stored in a vector database or search index.

→ When a user asks a question, the system retrieves relevant chunks using vector search, keyword search or both.

→ The LLM receives the question and retrieved context, then generates an answer with references where possible.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 13. Character-based vs word-based vs semantic chunking

→ Character-based chunking splits text according to a fixed number of characters.

→ Word-based chunking splits text based on word count, but it may separate related ideas.

→ Semantic chunking tries to keep related sentences or concepts together.

→ I would evaluate retrieval quality and cost before choosing the strategy because no single approach works best for every document.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 14. Vector search vs hybrid search

→ Vector search finds content based on semantic similarity, even when the wording differs.

→ Keyword search is useful for exact terms, account numbers, part numbers and identifiers.

→ Hybrid search combines semantic and keyword search to improve retrieval across different query types.

→ I would evaluate precision and recall using real questions before selecting the final approach.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 15. How do you reduce hallucinations in an LLM application?

→ I provide relevant retrieved context and instruct the model not to invent information.

→ I use citations, output validation and an abstention response when evidence is insufficient.

→ I test with known-answer questions, unanswerable questions and edge cases.

→ I measure errors using a defined evaluation dataset instead of claiming an unsupported accuracy percentage.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 16. How do you protect sensitive data when using AI?

→ I remove or mask passwords, tokens, account details and personal information before sending data to an approved AI service.

→ I provide only the minimum information required for the task.

→ I use access controls, encryption and audit logging where appropriate.

→ I review AI output and never expose confidential information through generated responses or logs.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 3: CORE JAVA FUNDAMENTALS

## 17. Why is Java platform-independent?

→ Java source code is compiled into bytecode.

→ Bytecode runs on a Java Virtual Machine rather than directly on the operating system.

→ Each operating system has its own compatible JVM implementation.

→ Therefore, the same compiled bytecode can run on different platforms with a compatible JVM.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 18. Explain JVM memory areas

→ Heap stores objects and arrays and is shared between threads.

→ Each thread has its own stack, containing method frames and local variables.

→ Metaspace stores class metadata.

→ Garbage collection reclaims unreachable heap objects, while excessive recursion can cause StackOverflowError.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 19. Difference between `==` and `.equals()`

→ For primitives, `==` compares values; for references, it compares object identity.

→ `.equals()` compares logical equality when the class implements it appropriately.

→ `"Vikas".equals("VIKAS")` returns `false` because String comparison is case-sensitive.

→ For String content comparison, use `.equals()` or `.equalsIgnoreCase()` when case-insensitive comparison is required.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 20. Why do we override `equals()` and `hashCode()` together?

→ Hash-based collections use `hashCode()` to identify a bucket.

→ They use `equals()` to check whether keys are logically equal.

→ If two objects are equal according to `equals()`, they must return the same hash code.

→ If only `equals()` is overridden, collections such as HashMap may behave incorrectly for logically equal objects.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 21. What is immutability?

→ An immutable object cannot change its observable state after construction.

→ String is an example of an immutable Java class.

→ Immutability makes objects easier to share safely between threads and reduces unexpected side effects.

→ To create an immutable class, use controlled construction, private final fields and no methods that expose mutable internal state.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 22. What is StringBuilder?

→ StringBuilder is a mutable class used to build or modify strings.

→ It is generally more efficient than repeated String concatenation inside a loop.

→ It is not thread-safe by default.

→ String is preferable for immutable text, while StringBuilder is useful for repeated string modifications in a single thread.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 23. What is a Singleton class?

→ Singleton ensures one instance within its intended scope.

→ In Spring, a bean is singleton-scoped by default within an application context.

→ Java can implement a robust singleton using an enum, for example `enum AppConfig { INSTANCE }`.

→ Singleton scope does not automatically make mutable fields thread-safe.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 24. Method overloading vs overriding

→ Overloading means the same method name with different parameter lists.

→ Overloading is resolved at compile time.

→ Overriding means a subclass provides a compatible implementation of an inherited instance method.

→ Overriding uses runtime polymorphism; return type alone cannot be used to overload a method.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 25. What is a default method in an interface?

→ A default method provides an implementation inside an interface using the `default` keyword.

→ It allows an interface to add behaviour without forcing every existing implementation to implement the new method.

→ It is useful when the default behaviour genuinely belongs to the interface contract.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 26. What is Optional?

→ Optional represents a value that may or may not be present.

→ It helps make absence explicit, especially in return types.

→ Methods such as `map()`, `orElse()`, `orElseGet()` and `ifPresent()` help process optional values.

→ Avoid calling `get()` without checking whether the value is present.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 27. `transient` vs `@Transient`

→ Java's `transient` keyword excludes a field from default Java object serialization.

→ JPA's `@Transient` excludes a field from database persistence mapping.

→ `Serializable` is a marker interface used by Java's object serialization mechanism.

→ For REST responses, use DTOs that exclude passwords and sensitive fields instead of relying only on serialization settings.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 28. Records vs POJOs

→ A record is a concise Java data carrier with generated constructor, accessors, `equals()`, `hashCode()` and `toString()`.

→ Record components are final, but referenced mutable objects can still be changed.

→ Records are useful for API request and response DTOs.

→ They are generally not suitable as JPA entities because entities often require mutable state and proxy-compatible construction.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 29. Checked vs unchecked exceptions

→ Checked exceptions must be caught or declared, such as IOException.

→ Unchecked exceptions extend RuntimeException, such as NullPointerException and IllegalArgumentException.

→ A checked exception is not necessarily thrown at compile time; the compiler checks whether it is handled or declared.

→ Handle an exception where recovery is possible; otherwise, propagate it to a suitable exception handler.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 4: COLLECTIONS

## 30. ArrayList vs LinkedList vs HashMap

→ ArrayList provides fast indexed access and is suitable for iteration and reading many elements.

→ LinkedList can insert or remove efficiently when the node is already located, but finding an index takes O(n).

→ HashMap stores key-value pairs and provides average O(1) lookup under normal hash distribution.

→ Choose a collection based on access patterns, memory usage, ordering and thread-safety requirements.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 31. How does HashMap work internally?

→ HashMap calculates a hash from the key and uses it to select a bucket.

→ When multiple keys map to the same bucket, a collision occurs.

→ Equal keys replace the previous value, while duplicate values are allowed.

→ When the load threshold is reached, HashMap resizes; collision-heavy buckets may become tree bins in modern Java implementations.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 32. HashMap vs Hashtable vs ConcurrentHashMap

→ HashMap is not thread-safe and allows one null key and multiple null values.

→ Hashtable is a legacy synchronised map and does not allow null keys or values.

→ ConcurrentHashMap supports concurrent operations using finer-grained coordination.

→ Use ConcurrentHashMap for shared concurrent map access, but remember that multi-step business operations may still require atomic methods or additional locking.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 5: JAVA 8+, STREAMS AND FUNCTIONAL INTERFACES

## 33. What are the advantages of Streams?

→ Streams provide a declarative way to filter, transform, sort and aggregate data.

→ They support lazy intermediate operations and functional-style processing.

→ They can improve readability, but they are not automatically faster than loops.

→ For very large datasets, benchmark loops and streams using realistic data before deciding.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 34. `map()` vs `filter()` vs `flatMap()`

→ `filter()` keeps elements that satisfy a condition.

→ `map()` transforms each element into one result.

→ `flatMap()` transforms elements into streams and flattens the results into one stream.

→ These operations do not modify the original collection unless the supplied functions themselves cause side effects.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 35. Intermediate vs terminal operations

→ Intermediate operations include `filter()`, `map()` and `sorted()`.

→ They return another stream and are generally lazy.

→ Terminal operations include `collect()`, `count()`, `reduce()` and `forEach()`.

→ A terminal operation triggers processing, and a stream should generally be consumed only once.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 36. Explain functional interfaces

→ A functional interface has one abstract method.

→ Predicate accepts a value and returns a boolean.

→ Function transforms an input into an output; Consumer accepts input without returning a result.

→ Supplier provides a value without taking input; Runnable executes a task without returning a result.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 37. Java 8 vs Java 17 vs Java 21

→ Java 8 introduced lambdas, Streams, Optional and the modern Date/Time API.

→ Java 17 includes records, sealed classes and pattern matching for `instanceof`.

→ Java 21 includes virtual threads, record patterns and pattern matching for `switch`.

→ Mention features you have actually used and verify the exact features of any newer Java release before discussing them.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 6: MULTITHREADING AND CONCURRENCY

## 38. `synchronized` vs `volatile` vs AtomicInteger

→ `synchronized` provides mutual exclusion and visibility for a critical section.

→ `volatile` provides visibility and ordering guarantees, but does not make `count++` atomic.

→ AtomicInteger provides atomic operations such as `incrementAndGet()`.

→ Choose based on whether you need visibility, a single atomic operation or protection around multiple related operations.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 39. How does ConcurrentHashMap work?

→ ConcurrentHashMap is designed to support concurrent reads and updates.

→ It uses techniques such as CAS and synchronisation around affected bins rather than one global lock for every operation.

→ It prevents concurrent map operations from corrupting the map structure.

→ However, it does not automatically make mutable values or multi-step business logic thread-safe.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 40. What is a race condition?

→ A race condition occurs when the result depends on the timing or interleaving of concurrent operations.

→ For example, two threads may both check that a key is absent before either inserts it.

→ Use atomic operations such as `putIfAbsent()`, `computeIfAbsent()` or `merge()` where appropriate.

→ For business-critical updates, use proper database transactions, constraints or locking.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 41. ExecutorService vs ThreadPoolExecutor

→ ExecutorService provides an API for submitting tasks and managing execution.

→ ThreadPoolExecutor is a configurable implementation with controls over worker threads, queues and rejection policies.

→ Fixed pools limit worker count; cached pools may grow significantly; virtual-thread-per-task executors suit many blocking I/O workloads.

→ Always consider resource limits, task timeouts, queue capacity and executor shutdown.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 42. What are virtual threads?

→ Virtual threads are lightweight threads managed by the JVM.

→ They are useful for applications with many concurrent blocking I/O operations.

→ They do not automatically improve CPU-heavy processing.

→ Database connections, external API limits and CPU capacity remain constraints even when using virtual threads.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 43. How do you wait for five parallel API calls?

→ Start independent calls using CompletableFuture and a suitable executor.

→ Use `CompletableFuture.allOf(f1, f2, f3, f4, f5).join()` to wait for completion.

→ Retrieve each result after completion and handle exceptions and timeouts.

→ Use an explicit executor when resource management and predictable concurrency are important.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 44. How do you handle concurrency in banking transactions?

→ Use database transactions, constraints and suitable optimistic or pessimistic locking.

→ Use idempotency keys to prevent duplicate processing of the same business request.

→ Keep account or ledger updates atomic and maintain durable transaction status.

→ For cross-service workflows, use Saga, reliable messaging and reconciliation rather than relying only on in-memory locks.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 7: JVM, MEMORY AND GARBAGE COLLECTION

## 45. How does garbage collection work?

→ Garbage collection reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable.

→ Heap memory stores objects, while each thread has its own stack.

→ If the JVM cannot allocate memory after garbage collection, it may throw OutOfMemoryError.

→ Excessive recursion can cause StackOverflowError; explicit calls to `System.gc()` are not a normal memory-management solution.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 46. How do you debug OutOfMemoryError in production?

→ Check heap usage, garbage-collection logs, allocation rate and container memory metrics.

→ Capture a heap dump safely and inspect retained objects using tools such as Eclipse MAT or JVisualVM.

→ Look for unbounded caches, retained collections, large payloads and resource leaks.

→ Fix the root cause, then validate the solution under representative load instead of only increasing heap size.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 47. How do you process a 5 GB JSON file?

→ Do not load the entire file into memory as one String or object tree.

→ Use Jackson streaming APIs to process JSON elements incrementally.

→ Validate and persist records in bounded batches.

→ Track progress and make the process restartable so failures do not require starting from the beginning.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 8: SOLID PRINCIPLES AND DESIGN PATTERNS

## 48. Explain SOLID principles

→ Single Responsibility: a class should have one main reason to change.

→ Open/Closed: extend behaviour without repeatedly modifying stable code.

→ Liskov Substitution: implementations must honour the contract of their parent type.

→ Interface Segregation and Dependency Inversion: prefer focused interfaces and depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 49. What is Clean Code?

→ Clean Code is readable, maintainable code with clear names, small responsibilities and simple control flow.

→ It is not a design pattern.

→ Design patterns are reusable solutions to recurring design problems.

→ I focus on meaningful names, low coupling, tests and avoiding unnecessary complexity.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 50. Which design patterns have you used?

→ Strategy is useful when different implementations provide the same business capability.

→ Factory centralises object creation when the concrete type varies.

→ Builder improves construction of objects with many optional fields.

→ Singleton provides one shared instance within a defined scope; in Spring, singleton bean scope often handles this requirement.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 51. One endpoint serves India and Singapore with different downstream APIs. How would you design it?

→ Keep shared request validation and common business rules in one service.

→ Define a country-specific strategy interface, such as `CountryProcessor`.

→ Implement separate processors for India and Singapore and register them using a country-code map.

→ When adding another country, add a new implementation rather than modifying a large `if/else` block.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 9: SPRING CORE AND DEPENDENCY INJECTION

## 52. What are IoC and Dependency Injection?

→ Inversion of Control means the framework manages object creation and lifecycle.

→ Dependency Injection supplies the dependencies required by a class.

→ Spring supports constructor, setter and field injection.

→ Constructor injection is generally preferred for required dependencies because it makes dependencies explicit and improves unit testing.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 53. Is constructor injection more tightly coupled than field injection?

→ Constructor injection does not inherently create stronger coupling; it makes dependencies explicit.

→ Depend on interfaces when alternative implementations are required.

→ Required dependencies can be declared as final fields.

→ If a constructor has too many dependencies, consider whether the class has too many responsibilities.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 54. `@Qualifier` vs `@Primary`

→ `@Primary` selects the preferred bean when multiple candidates exist.

→ `@Qualifier` identifies a specific bean at an injection point.

→ Both help Spring resolve ambiguity between multiple implementations.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 55. Explain the Spring bean lifecycle

→ Spring creates the bean and injects its dependencies.

→ Aware callbacks and bean post-processors run where applicable.

→ Initialisation callbacks execute before the bean is ready for use.

→ Destruction callbacks can run when the application context closes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 56. `@Component` vs `@Service` vs `@Repository`

→ `@Component` is a general Spring-managed component.

→ `@Service` identifies service-layer logic.

→ `@Repository` identifies persistence components and can participate in exception translation.

→ `@Controller` handles MVC requests, while `@RestController` returns response bodies by default.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 57. How do you resolve circular dependencies?

→ A circular dependency occurs when bean A depends on B and B depends on A.

→ Constructor-based circular dependencies generally fail during context creation.

→ I would first refactor responsibilities or extract common logic into another component.

→ Events or dependency inversion may remove the cycle; `@Lazy` should be considered a workaround, not the default solution.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 10: SPRING BOOT AND CONFIGURATION

## 58. What is Spring Boot?

→ Spring Boot builds on Spring and reduces configuration effort.

→ It provides starter dependencies, auto-configuration and embedded server support.

→ It supports externalised configuration and production monitoring through Actuator.

→ It does not remove the need to understand Spring fundamentals.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 59. What does `@SpringBootApplication` contain?

→ `@SpringBootConfiguration` identifies the configuration class.

→ `@EnableAutoConfiguration` enables conditional automatic configuration.

→ `@ComponentScan` discovers components within the configured scanning scope.

→ Together, these annotations simplify application setup.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 60. How does auto-configuration work?

→ Spring Boot checks the classpath, application properties and existing beans.

→ It creates suitable default beans when the required conditions are met.

→ Custom beans can override defaults where the configuration supports it.

→ Conditions such as `@ConditionalOnProperty` allow configuration to depend on a property value.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 61. How do you manage dev, SIT, UAT and production configuration?

→ Keep common defaults in `application.properties` or `application.yml`.

→ Use files such as `application-dev.yml` and `application-prod.yml` for environment-specific settings.

→ Activate the appropriate profile through environment configuration or deployment settings.

→ Keep secrets in an approved secrets manager and verify property precedence during deployment.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 62. How do you disable Redis using configuration?

→ Put Redis configuration in a separate configuration class.

→ Use `@ConditionalOnProperty` to enable the Redis beans only when the property is enabled.

→ Provide an alternative implementation or clear failure behaviour when caching is disabled.

→ Keep the controller independent of the Redis implementation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 63. How do you improve Spring Boot startup time?

→ Measure startup time before changing configuration.

→ Reduce unnecessary component scanning and avoid expensive initialisation where possible.

→ Move non-essential loading out of synchronous startup work.

→ Use startup profiling and verify that optimisation does not affect readiness or correctness.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 11: JPA, HIBERNATE AND TRANSACTIONS

## 64. JPA vs Hibernate

→ JPA is a persistence specification.

→ Hibernate is a widely used implementation of JPA.

→ A Hibernate dialect helps generate database-specific SQL.

→ JPA repositories simplify persistence operations, but query design and transaction boundaries still require careful attention.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 65. How does `@Transactional` work?

→ Spring generally manages transactions through proxies around Spring-managed beans.

→ Database operations within the transaction can commit together or roll back according to the configured rules.

→ By default, unchecked exceptions trigger rollback; checked exceptions usually do not unless configured.

→ Self-invocation may bypass proxy-based transaction interception, so transaction boundaries must be designed carefully.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 66. Debit succeeds but credit fails. How do you handle it?

→ If both operations are in the same database transaction, allow the failure to trigger rollback.

→ If the operations span separate services or databases, a single local transaction cannot guarantee atomicity.

→ Use Saga, durable event processing and compensating actions for distributed workflows.

→ For money transfers, also use idempotency, transaction status tracking and reconciliation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 67. What is the N+1 query problem?

→ N+1 occurs when one query loads a list and additional queries are executed for related data for each item.

→ It increases database round trips and can make an API slow.

→ I use query inspection, projections, fetch joins, EntityGraph or batch fetching as appropriate.

→ I verify the generated SQL and measure performance instead of applying eager loading everywhere.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 12: CONCURRENCY CONTROL AND LOCKING

## 68. Optimistic vs pessimistic locking

→ Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are relatively uncommon and uses a version field to detect conflicting updates.

→ Pessimistic locking acquires database locks to prevent conflicting operations while a transaction is active.

→ Optimistic locking is useful for lower-contention workloads.

→ Pessimistic locking may be useful for high-contention critical updates, but can cause blocking and deadlocks.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 69. How does `@Version` prevent lost updates?

→ Two requests read the same database version.

→ The first request updates the row and increments the version.

→ The second request attempts to update using the old version, so the update fails.

→ The second request must re-read the latest state and re-evaluate the business operation before retrying.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 70. Why is Redis plus a transaction ID not enough?

→ A transaction ID helps identify a request and can support idempotency.

→ Redis alone does not guarantee that concurrent database updates cannot overwrite each other.

→ Use database transactions, unique constraints and optimistic or pessimistic locking for data consistency.

→ Use Redis for coordination only when its failure and distributed-locking semantics are properly understood.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 13: REST API DESIGN

## 71. How do you build a REST API from scratch?

→ Define the resource, HTTP method, request DTO, response DTO and status codes.

→ Implement controller, service, repository, validation and transaction handling.

→ Add authentication, authorisation, logging, metrics and global exception handling.

→ Test success cases, invalid input, missing resources, conflicts and downstream failures.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 72. PUT vs PATCH

→ PUT generally replaces the resource representation.

→ PATCH applies a partial update.

→ Both require authorisation and validation.

→ Choose the method based on the intended update semantics and document the API contract clearly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 73. `@RequestBody` vs `@PathVariable` vs `@RequestParam`

→ `@RequestBody` binds data from the request body, commonly JSON.

→ `@PathVariable` binds a value from the URL path, such as `/users/10`.

→ `@RequestParam` binds query parameters, such as `/users?status=ACTIVE`.

→ Use each annotation according to the API's resource and filtering requirements.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 74. How do you design user CRUD APIs?

→ `POST /users` creates a user.

→ `GET /users/{id}` retrieves a user; `GET /users` lists users with pagination.

→ `PUT` or `PATCH /users/{id}` updates a user, and `DELETE /users/{id}` deletes or deactivates one.

→ Use DTOs, validation, authorisation and consistent error responses; never expose passwords or password hashes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 75. How do you maintain backward compatibility?

→ Prefer additive changes, such as introducing an optional field.

→ Avoid changing the meaning or type of an existing field unexpectedly.

→ Use versioning when a breaking change cannot be avoided.

→ Keep old consumers working during migration and deprecate old behaviour through a defined plan.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 14: VALIDATION AND EXCEPTION HANDLING

## 76. How do you validate an incoming request?

→ Use Bean Validation annotations such as `@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Size` and `@Positive`.

→ Add `@Valid` to the request body parameter.

→ Put business-specific validation in the service or domain layer.

→ Frontend validation improves usability, but backend validation is always required.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 77. What is `@RestControllerAdvice`?

→ It handles exceptions across multiple REST controllers.

→ It allows the application to return consistent error responses.

→ It can map validation errors, not-found exceptions and business conflicts to suitable HTTP status codes.

→ It prevents every controller from implementing its own duplicate exception-handling logic.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 78. What is the difference between 401, 403, 404 and 500?

→ 401 means authentication is missing or invalid.

→ 403 means the caller is authenticated but lacks permission.

→ 404 means the requested resource was not found.

→ 500 means an unexpected server-side error occurred; use specific 4xx or 5xx codes when appropriate.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 15: SECURITY, JWT AND OAUTH2

## 79. Explain JWT authentication

→ The user authenticates with an authentication server or identity provider.

→ The server issues a signed JWT containing claims and an expiry time.

→ The client sends the token in the `Authorization: Bearer` header.

→ The resource server validates the signature, issuer, audience, expiry and required claims before authorising the request.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 80. What happens when a JWT expires?

→ The server rejects the expired token, normally with HTTP 401.

→ If refresh tokens are supported, the client can request a new access token according to the authentication flow.

→ If refresh fails, the user must authenticate again.

→ The frontend should handle expiry gracefully without creating an infinite retry loop.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 81. How do you implement role-based authorisation?

→ Authentication identifies the user.

→ Authorisation checks whether the user has permission to perform the requested operation.

→ Configure route-level rules and method-level checks such as `@PreAuthorize`.

→ Also verify resource ownership so a user cannot access another user's record by changing an ID.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 82. How do you secure communication between microservices?

→ Use TLS and an appropriate service identity or token-based authentication mechanism.

→ Apply least-privilege permissions and validate token issuer, audience and scope.

→ Store credentials in a secrets manager and rotate them according to policy.

→ Propagate user tokens only when the downstream service is intended to accept them.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 16: MICROSERVICES, RESILIENCE AND SAGA

## 83. Monolith vs microservices

→ A monolith deploys the application as one unit.

→ Microservices divide business capabilities into independently deployable services.

→ Microservices can improve independent scaling and ownership.

→ They also introduce network failures, distributed tracing, deployment complexity and data-consistency challenges.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 84. How do microservices communicate?

→ REST is suitable for synchronous request-response communication.

→ Kafka is suitable for asynchronous events and decoupled consumers.

→ Feign provides a declarative HTTP client; other options include WebClient and suitable Java HTTP clients.

→ Add timeouts, authentication, tracing, retries and clear contracts.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 85. What is an API Gateway?

→ An API Gateway provides a common entry point for client requests.

→ It can route requests and apply authentication, rate limiting and other cross-cutting policies.

→ Business logic should remain in the appropriate service rather than being placed entirely in the gateway.

→ The gateway does not replace service-level authorisation or resilience.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 86. What is a Circuit Breaker?

→ Closed state allows requests while failures are monitored.

→ Open state blocks calls temporarily after a failure threshold is reached.

→ Half-open state allows limited trial calls to check whether the dependency has recovered.

→ Fallbacks must be safe and must not report a failed financial operation as successful.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 87. What if a downstream service is unavailable?

→ Configure connection and read timeouts.

→ Retry transient failures with bounded exponential backoff and jitter.

→ Use a circuit breaker to prevent repeatedly calling an unhealthy dependency.

→ For asynchronous processing, use a DLQ and controlled replay for messages that cannot be processed after bounded retries.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 88. What is the Saga pattern?

→ Saga manages a business transaction that spans multiple services.

→ Each service performs a local transaction and publishes an event or returns a result.

→ If a later step fails, a compensating action may reverse or counteract an earlier business operation.

→ Orchestration uses a coordinator; choreography uses events to trigger the next steps.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 89. How do you prevent duplicate payments?

→ Require an idempotency key for the payment request.

→ Store the key and transaction result durably, enforcing uniqueness in the database.

→ Use transactions and suitable locking for account or ledger updates.

→ For cross-service operations, maintain durable status and reconciliation so retries do not cause double debit or credit.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 17: DISTRIBUTED TRACING AND LOGGING

## 90. How do you find which microservice is failing?

→ Start with the trace ID or correlation ID for the failed request.

→ Inspect the distributed trace to identify the slow or failing service span.

→ Compare logs, error rates, latency, database metrics and downstream calls.

→ Confirm the root cause and monitor the service after applying the fix.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 91. What is a correlation ID?

→ A correlation ID identifies a request across multiple services.

→ It is passed through HTTP headers and message headers.

→ Structured logs include the ID so related events can be found together.

→ A distributed trace ID and span IDs provide more detailed timing and parent-child relationships between operations.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 18: KAFKA AND MESSAGING

## 92. Why do you use Kafka?

→ Kafka supports asynchronous event-driven communication between services.

→ It allows events to be retained and replayed according to topic retention settings.

→ Multiple consumer groups can process the same topic independently.

→ In LabFlow, Kafka supports laboratory-result event processing and service integration.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 93. Explain topics, partitions, offsets and consumer groups

→ A topic is a named stream of records.

→ Partitions divide a topic and enable parallel processing.

→ An offset identifies a record's position within a partition.

→ Consumers in the same group share partition assignments, while different groups can independently consume the same topic.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 94. What is the difference between at-most-once, at-least-once and exactly-once?

→ At-most-once processing can lose records but avoids normal redelivery.

→ At-least-once processing can redeliver records, so consumers should be idempotent.

→ Kafka exactly-once features provide guarantees within supported Kafka processing boundaries.

→ External database or API side effects still require appropriate transaction and idempotency design.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 95. How do you handle duplicate Kafka events?

→ Give each event a stable identifier.

→ Store processed identifiers or enforce a unique business key in the database.

→ Make the consumer idempotent so processing the same event again does not repeat the business effect.

→ Use transactions or an outbox pattern where required to keep database state and event publication reliable.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 96. How do you handle out-of-order Kafka events?

→ Partition events by a key when ordering for that key is required.

→ Include a version, sequence number or event timestamp where appropriate.

→ Compare the incoming event with the stored state and reject or defer stale updates according to business rules.

→ Do not assume global ordering across Kafka partitions.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 97. What if a Kafka consumer stops processing?

→ Check consumer-group status, partition assignment, lag and committed offsets.

→ Check authentication, deserialisation errors, poison messages and downstream timeouts.

→ Inspect whether a record is repeatedly failing or the consumer is blocked.

→ Recover safely using bounded retries, DLQ handling and idempotent replay.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 98. What is a Dead Letter Queue?

→ A DLQ stores messages that cannot be processed after the configured retry policy.

→ It prevents a poison message from blocking normal processing indefinitely.

→ Monitoring should alert when the DLQ grows.

→ After fixing the root cause, messages can be replayed through a controlled process.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 99. What is the Transactional Outbox pattern?

→ The application saves the business change and an event record in the same database transaction.

→ A separate publisher reads unpublished outbox records and sends them to Kafka.

→ The event can be published again if acknowledgement or status updating fails, so consumers should remain idempotent.

→ This prevents the common failure where the database commits but the application crashes before publishing the event.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 19: CACHING

## 100. How do you implement caching in Spring Boot?

→ Use `@Cacheable` for caching results, `@CachePut` for updating a cache entry and `@CacheEvict` for removing entries.

→ Choose cache keys carefully and configure TTL and eviction policies.

→ Cache data that can tolerate a defined level of staleness.

→ For critical financial data, keep the database or ledger as the source of truth.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 101. Redis vs local caching

→ Redis is a distributed cache shared across application instances.

→ Local caches such as Caffeine are usually faster for in-process access but are not automatically shared.

→ Distributed caches introduce network latency and operational dependencies.

→ Choose based on consistency, sharing requirements, latency and operational cost.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 102. How do you handle stale cache data?

→ Update or invalidate the cache after a successful database commit.

→ Use an outbox or reliable event to propagate cache invalidation when needed.

→ TTL provides a maximum cache lifetime but does not guarantee immediate consistency.

→ Never let a stale cached balance silently authorise a financial transaction.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 103. What is an LRU cache?

→ LRU means Least Recently Used.

→ When the cache reaches capacity, it removes the entry that has not been accessed for the longest time.

→ Java's LinkedHashMap supports access-order mode and can override `removeEldestEntry()`.

→ A simple LinkedHashMap implementation is not thread-safe by default.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SECTION 20: DATABASES AND SQL

## 104. INNER JOIN vs LEFT JOIN vs RIGHT JOIN

→ INNER JOIN returns rows with matching records on both sides.

→ LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table.

→ RIGHT JOIN returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table.

→ Choose the join according to which records must remain in the result.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 105. Why should we not index every column?

→ Indexes can improve selective reads and joins.

→ Every index consumes storage and adds work to inserts, updates and deletes.

→ I choose indexes based on query patterns, selectivity and execution plans.

→ I validate the result using EXPLAIN or EXPLAIN ANALYZE where supported.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 106. How do you optimise a slow SQL query?

→ Inspect the actual query plan and identify scans, expensive joins, sorts and incorrect row estimates.

→ Check indexes, statistics, filters, joins and the number of rows returned.

→ Avoid unnecessary columns and use pagination or projections where appropriate.

→ Compare performance with realistic production-sized data and measure the improvement.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 107. How do you prevent duplicate database records?

→ Identify the business key that defines uniqueness.

→ Enforce that rule using a database unique constraint.

→ Use idempotent inserts, upserts or conflict handling where supported.

→ Do not rely only on checking whether a record exists before inserting because concurrent requests can race.

